1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
One sky. The Big Dipper isn’t a
constellation.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:11,000
The sky is split into 88 constellations.
Many shapes you spot first aren’t on the
list.

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:17,000
The Big Dipper is just the Great Bear’s
rear and tail.

4
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:32,000
In China, though, Beidou (Northern
Dipper) is a star group of its own,
marking the seasons and directions.

5
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:38,000
Vega, Altair and Deneb sit in three
different constellations: Lyra, Aquila
and Cygnus.

6
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:50,000
Vega comes from Arabic for “the swooping
eagle”; Altair, “the flying eagle”.

7
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:58,000
In Arabia, each eagle has two small
stars — an asterism of its own. Deneb:
“the hen’s tail”.

8
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:06,000
Northern sky, late evening: the Summer
Triangle climbs in the east. Northwest:
the Big Dipper, handle up.

9
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:13,000
Follow the handle’s curve west to
Arcturus, then Spica — the Arc to
Arcturus.

10
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:17,000
The Milky Way: a river, a road, a seam.

11
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:20,000
Follow for a new sky every week.

